**TL;DR:** Managed *Azure Database for MySQL* cost us 50%–80% in raw query time compared with a self‑hosted MariaDB VM. After six months of pain, we flipped back to a classic VM and instantly halved our page‑load times. If you run a transaction‑heavy Drupal (or WordPress or similar) site, steer clear of Azure's managed MySQL unless you enjoy 503s and coffee‑fuelled debugging nights.

---

## Falling into a managed‑DB trap

In late 2024 our small web team moved our large Drupal 10 site over to a recommended standard of Azure cloud hosting. We followed best practice recommendation for the *"fully managed, worry‑free MySQL at cloud scale!"*

Reality begged to differ. Those ambition stretched into stress‑filled weeks. Editors grumbled that the admin theme now loaded "slower than dial‑up", cron jobs quietly died, and New Relic lit up like a retro equaliser.

As seasoned Drupal folk we blamed everything except the new database: contrib modules, PHP OpCache, custom code, back we just couldn't rule out that something was *off* in the DB's performance.

### Non‑cached pages: the early smoke signal

Early in the migration process there were large warning signs, anything that was not already in cache felt like mud:

* **/news view filter** – searching for *"UNDRR"* on Azure took **5.8 s server response** versus **1.68 s on prod** (same code, same data).
* Editor paths like `/admin/content?type=conference` routinely doubled in TTFB, new‑node forms crawled past 13 s, and the media modal kept engineers on hold for eight.

Pressure to finalise a migration was high and we -- in hindsight, mistakingly -- agreed to move environments and either fix the issue later. I still had not given up hope that this was purely a matter of priming the site and that once the site was lively, it would be more responsive.

I was wrong.

### How we measured the pain

As the weeks wore on, the performance issues continue, and we needed to track down where the pain was coming from.

* Enabled Drupal 10's **Performance Profiler** with query logging at `watchdog.DEBUG`.
* Compiled 20 top‑traffic URLs plus 10 admin paths into **JMeter** scripts (50 concurrent users, 5‑minute ramp‑up).
* Alternated load tests hourly between the managed MySQL endpoint and the MariaDB VM to neutralise cache effects.

The numbers were grim: aggregate query time was **51% slower** on Azure DB for MySQL, with p95 latency brushing three seconds. PHP execution time barely budged, confirming the real villain sat behind that "fully managed" façade.

At this point, we could prove that the production environment's database was too slow, but we could point out why.

### A diversion in the quest: And Admin Menu's four‑level query avalanche

Our early profiling runs were skewed by Drupal's **admin_menu** module, whose convenience hides an ugly truth: it fires off dozens of queries on every backend request. That overhead clouded our view as we hunted for Azure's real bottleneck. (We're rolling our own slim, in‑house admin menu to fix this for good — details to come.)

Drupal's trusty **admin_menu** contrib module politely renders drop‑downs by loading the entire menu tree—**four levels deep**—on every backend page. On a chunky local database that's fine; under Azure's network‑hobbled MySQL it was like adding ankle weights to a marathon runner.

* **+117 extra SELECTs per request.** We counted.
* Backend TTFB spiked from **1.1 s → 3.2 s** when admin_menu was enabled.

Our quick win during diagnostics was simply **setting admin_menu to two levels of depth**, which immediately shaved ~2 s off critical admin pages and proved the DB layer was already buckling.

We have a more holsitic forthcoming fix by rolling our own admin toolbar that is much simpler in functionality and shows only the links our admins and editors need through a simple taxonomy system (watch for a future post here ...)

## Measuring raw performance

To isolate this we took our Drupal configuration out of the equation and ran a number of queries to calculate performance between the **Azure Database for MySQL** with two alternatives: **MySQL** and **MariaDB** running on dedicated Azure virtual machines. Across every database-heavy test—including admin pages, content listings, and background operations—the **VM-based databases crushed the baseline**, slashing load times by up to 60%.

The standout winner was **MariaDB on a VM**, which consistently outperformed both the current setup and VM-based MySQL. Even MySQL on a VM delivered big gains over the managed database, but MariaDB edged ahead in nearly every case, especially on complex pages. Here's a snapshot of the results:

| Test Scenario | Azure MySQL (Baseline) | VM MySQL | VM MariaDB | Best Performer |
|---------------|------------------------|----------|------------|----------------|
| DB01 (Heavy Query) | 27.0 sec | 18.4 sec | **10.8 sec** | MariaDB |
| PP02 (DB Benchmark) | 4.36 sec | 3.35 sec | **2.24 sec** | MariaDB |
| UI01 (Admin Page) | 47.5 sec | 40.35 sec | **40.29 sec** | MariaDB |
| UI02 (Cached Admin) | 0.54 sec | 0.53 sec | **0.41 sec** | MariaDB |
| ME01 Cold (Content) | 50.6 sec | 42.0 sec | **41.8 sec** | MariaDB |
| ME01 Warm (Content) | 1.15 sec | 1.00 sec | **1.01 sec** | MariaDB |

A massive hat tip to Johan for orchestrating the load tests, crunching the stats, and getting us the raw data we needed to conclusevely move away from what was billed as best practice.

## Making and measuring the move

It took quite a bit of planning, approvals, doing and testing, but after several weeks we completed the move MariaDB. It was a win.

We ran Drupal 10's **Performance Profiler** module before and after the switch:

| Scenario | Azure MySQL (ms) | MariaDB VM (ms) | Δ |
|----------|------------------|-----------------|---|
| DB benchmark (total) | **5 434** | **2 636** | −51% |
| Admin » Appearance (cold cache) | 7 291 | 3 072 | −58% |
| Admin » Appearance (warm) | 3 008 | 698 | −77% |

> "Pure PHP stayed flat at ~0.67 s per request; all the wins came from faster queries." (internal test log)

Even end‑user stats told the same story: daily 503s dropped 77%, and anonymous page loads shaved ~1.7 s off the top.

As to why Azure Database for MySQL is so slow it's hard to say, it just seems not designed to work well for sprawling databases with thousands of transactions. If there's a secret lever we've missed, we'd love to hear it, as no ones been able to tell us nor have we researched one.

Bonus: [MariaDB is now the RDBMS recommended by Drupal 11, so](https://www.drupal.org/docs/getting-started/system-requirements/database-server-requirements#s-drupal-11) our pivot wasn't just pragmatic—it aligned us with core guidance.

## But what about PHP file reads?

Yes, PHP hits the file system on every request (even with OpCache). On Azure App Service those reads come from a remote SMB share. Benchmarks in the Azure OSS blog show a **100×** gap between local disk and the mounted `/home` volume under fio.

In our case Drupal's opcode cache masked most of that, so DB latency remained the dominant villain. Still, if you're shipping WordPress or Moodle you'll feel the sting sooner.

We still want to improve this, but for now it's not as crippling as the DB issues was.

## Take‑aways for Drupal and WordPress

1. **Benchmark first, migrate later.** Never agree to complete a migration when you have strong indicators of slow non-primed paths.
2. If you're planning on using Azure DB for MySQL, take extra care to benchmark thoroughly

